<p>The United States said Vice President JD Vance was prepared to travel to Islamabad for a second round of peace talks with Iran as a temporary ceasefire nears its expiry. Tehran, however, had not publicly committed to attend, and President Trump said he was unlikely to extend the truce if no deal is reached. Multiple outlets also reported that Trump said the United States would keep its blockade on Iranian ports and the Strait of Hormuz in place until a deal is made, while Iranian officials said they would not negotiate under threats and accused Washington of turning talks into a demand for surrender. Pakistan has emerged as the main diplomatic venue and says it is trying to bring both sides together. Some reports cite positive signals from Iran, even as Tehran&#x27;s public messaging remains mixed. The standoff matters well beyond the negotiating room: tension around Hormuz and the threat of renewed fighting have rattled oil and broader financial markets, while the still-open possibility of talks remains the clearest near-term path to avoiding a wider regional war.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/2qJvVMhSqavlDrHuTaBf_OjffiK-AflBJ88WoHIKZuunM_NQ1HTko6orHvRzbJ_6w3U4XNnSNeBqBsCwsX9S5snBRWqdC3tMuIjx-OjU5vLI' alt='A US Air Force Boeing C-17A makes final approach before landing at Nur Khan Airbase as Islamabad prepared for possible talks.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Ship seizure: Tehran accused Washington of &quot;maritime piracy&quot; after the U.S. seized an Iran-flagged vessel or tanker, adding another dispute just as diplomats tried to revive talks.</li><li>Islamabad security: Pakistan mounted an unprecedented security deployment in Islamabad as it prepared for possible multi-day negotiations involving senior U.S. officials and an Iranian team.</li><li>Domestic pressure: The New York Times reported that Iranian officials are balancing public defiance with private planning for talks because of pressure from hard-liners at home and longstanding mistrust of the United States.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Pakistan government: Pakistani officials say they are actively engaging both Tehran and Washington and remain confident they can bring Iran to Islamabad for talks despite the fluid situation. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/04/pakistan-confident-iran-will-attend-us-talks-senior-pakistani-government-source'>Al-Monitor</a>)</li><li>Iranian leadership: President Masoud Pezeshkian said there is deep historical mistrust toward the U.S. government, while other Iranian officials said Tehran would not negotiate under threat. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/apr/20/jd-vance-to-lead-us-delegation-in-pakistan-if-iran-agrees-to-talks'>The Guardian</a>, <a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/us-iran-war-news-hormuz-blockade-donald-trumps-bomb-vs-tehrans-cards-us-iran-trade-threats-as-truce-nears-end-11385951#publisher=newsstand'>NDTV</a>)</li><li>White House: The White House has projected confidence that talks can resume soon and said Vice President Vance is ready to return to Pakistan quickly for a second round. (<a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/next-round-of-u-s-iran-talks-in-doubt-as-tensions-rise-ahead-of-ceasefire-deadlinewar-with-iran-sot'>PBS NewsHour</a>)</li><li>France: Macron denounced the Hormuz blockade as diplomacy intensified, signaling European concern about escalation and trade disruption. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1993644/diplomatic-flurry-as-us-iran-truce-deadline-nears'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>China: President Xi told Saudi Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman that normal traffic through the Strait of Hormuz must continue, underscoring Beijing&#x27;s focus on shipping stability. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1993644/diplomatic-flurry-as-us-iran-truce-deadline-nears'>Dawn</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/next-round-of-u-s-iran-talks-in-doubt-as-tensions-rise-ahead-of-ceasefire-deadlinewar-with-ira